---
import InstrumentEmbed from "../../../components/lab/instrument/InstrumentEmbed.astro";
import BrandSliderComparison from "../../../components/studies/BrandSliderComparison.svelte";
import BurnStripComparison from "../../../components/studies/BurnStripComparison.svelte";
import InstrumentView from "../../../components/studies/InstrumentView.svelte";
import LabInstrument021 from "../../../components/studies/LabInstrument021.svelte";
import LabInstrumentConfigDemo023 from "../../../components/studies/LabInstrumentConfigDemo023.svelte";
import LabInstrumentConfigDemo027 from "../../../components/studies/LabInstrumentConfigDemo027.svelte";
import OptionToggleComparison from "../../../components/studies/OptionToggleComparison.svelte";
import PathToolComparison from "../../../components/studies/PathToolComparison.svelte";
import PathToolComparison028 from "../../../components/studies/PathToolComparison028.svelte";
import TraceGraphsExample from "../../../components/studies/TraceGraphsExample.svelte";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";
import { STARTER_DOCUMENT } from "../../../lib/instrument/starter";

const study = studies.find(
	(entry) => entry.slug === "lab-instrument-2026-11-01",
);
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
	<section aria-labelledby="problem">
		<h2 id="problem">01 / Every study builds its own ruler</h2>
		<p>
			A study that varies engine thrust and a study that varies hull mass both
			end up drawing a path, a speed curve and a few figures. Each builds that
			drawing itself, with its own axes, units and input program, so the two
			results cannot be laid side by side, and neither can be checked in the
			Lab. The work of measuring is repeated and the comparison is lost.
		</p>
		<p>
			The Lab instrument is one shared element that owns the measuring. A study
			hands it vehicle configurations and path sources; the instrument decides
			the views, scales, units, input program and controls. Paths are
			calculated, not flown, and drawn statically. The same element fills the
			container in the Lab and, in a study, takes the full column width and
			snaps to the centre of the reading flow. All controls sit in one
			collapsible pane that slides over the views.
		</p>
		<h3>The screen</h3>
		<p>
			The screen has two columns of equal width inside an even margin, with no
			header and no footer. The plan view takes the left column; the time-based
			diagrams take the right.
		</p>
		<h3>The control pane</h3>
		<ul>
			<li>
				Controls live where they act. Tools for reading the diagrams (time,
				scale, panning, showing a vehicle) sit on the diagrams themselves.
			</li>
			<li>
				The pane holds only the values under investigation: the settings a study
				varies and the document they are copied from. It is placed on the
				instrument's container, not drawn inside the scaled screen: anchored to
				the right edge, the container's full height, at a normal readable size.
			</li>
			<li>
				It is either visible or hidden, with no animation. When visible it sits
				over the screen; nothing behind it moves, resizes or reflows.
			</li>
			<li>
				It always starts hidden. Hidden, only a menu button in the container's
				top-right corner remains; visible, the pane's own close button takes
				that corner. Escape also closes it.
			</li>
			<li>
				Content taller than the screen scrolls inside the pane. Targets stay
				touch-sized.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<h3>The design problem: a configuration that travels</h3>
		<p>
			The instrument is only used if handing it work is easier than building a
			chart. That hand-over is a configuration: a small document an agent
			writes, the instrument draws, and the designer adjusts on screen. Say a
			plasma study wants faster acceleration: the designer changes that one
			value in the pane, compares it with the stock ship, and copies the
			resulting document back to the agent, or the agent reads it from the page,
			as the decided values. Nothing in it is mandatory; it names only what
			differs.
		</p>
		<p>
			So the questions are about that document: what a vehicle in it is, which
			parameters can change, which ships can be used, and how many vehicles one
			screen can hold. The game already describes ships as loadouts (a chassis
			with parts in fixed slots) and parts as parameter sets (mass, thrust,
			speed limit, response), which is a likely starting vocabulary. The default
			path scale, the slider as a design-system component and device readability
			checks sit outside this cycle.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section aria-labelledby="anatomy">
		<h2 id="anatomy">02.1 / Anatomy: three views, one readout, one pane</h2>
		<p>
			One fixed 16:10 screen holds a plan view of every path, three traces on a
			shared time axis with the input program beneath them, and a readout table.
			The table joins the moment (values at the slider time), the whole run
			(peak speed, time to 200 u/s, distance) and the configuration a study
			supplied, so an engine or hull comparison reads from the same calculation
			as the paths.
		</p>
		<div class="bleed">
			<LabInstrument021 client:load label="Lab instrument" />
		</div>
		<p class="limits">
			Fixture: four vehicles from two imagined studies, calculated with the
			free-rotation bench (2 aft + 2 side) on the shared 14-second input. The
			configuration columns show only the two settings that bench varies; real
			engine and hull properties are not connected. Samples are 1/60 s apart.
			The screen scales with its container; it has not been checked on an iPad.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section aria-labelledby="vehicle">
		<h2 id="vehicle">02.2 / A vehicle is a loadout plus what differs</h2>
		<p>
			Each vehicle in the configuration is a game loadout (a chassis with parts
			in its fixed slots, the same document the ship builder saves) plus an
			optional list of changes. Changes are grouped by engine family and name
			only the values that differ from the game, so a document that lists
			nothing describes ships exactly as they fly today.
		</p>
		<pre class="sample"><code>{`{
  "label": "Plasma aft, more thrust",
  "loadout": { "version": 2, "chassis": "balanced", "side": "ion",
               "stabilizers": true, "hull": [null, null],
               "aft": ["plasma", "plasma"] },
  "changes": { "plasma": { "thrust": 46000 } }
}`}</code></pre>
		<p>
			Changes belong to one vehicle, so the stock part and a changed one can be
			compared side by side. Because they are keyed by engine family, the values
			that should become the part's new definition can be read straight from the
			document.
		</p>
		<p>
			The working example is in 02.3, where it follows the control placement
			that came out of reviewing this one.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section aria-labelledby="placement">
		<h2 id="placement">
			02.3 / Reading tools on the diagrams, values in the pane
		</h2>
		<p>
			Each kind of control now sits where it acts. Time is scrubbed on the
			traces themselves (drag across them, or use the slider beneath); the plan
			view carries its own scale buttons; a vehicle is hidden or shown from its
			row in the readout. The pane opens to only the values under investigation
			and the document, so what it contains is exactly what gets copied back.
		</p>
		<p>
			In a study the instrument takes the full width and height of the window
			and snaps into place as it is scrolled to. It starts from a three-vehicle
			document: a Fighter with ion aft engines, the same with plasma, and plasma
			with more thrust. Change a value in the pane and watch the paths and
			readout follow.
		</p>
		<div class="bleed">
			<LabInstrumentConfigDemo023 client:load />
		</div>
		<p class="limits">
			Paths are calculated with the game's own engine function on the shared
			14-second input. Not included: gravity, power limits, and changes to part
			mass or chassis values, which that function does not take per vehicle. Ion
			values are shared by light and heavy ion and the stabilisers; the turn cap
			applies to every ship. Out-of-range values are refused rather than drawn.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section aria-labelledby="toggle">
		<h2 id="toggle">02.4 / A two-option toggle</h2>
		<p>
			The plan view's scale buttons choose one of two options, and the design
			system has no control for that yet. The toggle here is one button: its
			outer edge has the shared cut corners, and inside it is split straight
			down the middle into two equal halves. The selected half is filled like
			the shared filled button, so the selection reads with the same weight as a
			primary action.
		</p>
		<p>
			Two treatments of the unselected half were compared: inside a ghost
			button's lime border, or with no border as a faint fill. The faint fill
			without a border was chosen. It keeps the control quiet beside the
			diagrams and leaves lime for the selection alone.
		</p>
		<h3>Hover on the unselected half</h3>
		<p>
			The resting state works; the hover state did not, because darkening the
			faint fill made the half sink into the surface instead of answering the
			pointer. Three hover treatments are compared below: the fill brightens,
			the half previews a ghost button, or the shared selected-indicator bar
			appears along its bottom edge. A fourth, D, keeps the lift but swaps the
			semi-transparent white for semi-transparent lime, so hover borrows the
			brand colour at low strength while the selection keeps it at full. Each
			shows a live toggle to point at and the same hover state drawn without a
			pointer.
		</p>
		<OptionToggleComparison client:load />
		<h3>Accepted: brand tint</h3>
		<p>
			D was accepted. The two-option toggle is one control with the shared cut
			corners outside and a straight split inside. The selected half is filled
			lime; the unselected half is a faint white fill with a white label; on
			hover that fill turns to the same strength of lime (28%). The plan view's
			scale toggle in the current instrument, 02.7, uses it.
		</p>
		<p class="limits">
			Study-local: not in the shared button styles yet. Hover exists only with a
			mouse or trackpad; on the iPad a touch selects directly, so the resting
			and selected states carry the control there. Toggles with more than two
			options and on/off toggles are outside this comparison.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section aria-labelledby="slider">
		<h2 id="slider">02.5 / An on-brand slider</h2>
		<p>
			The instrument needs sliders in two places: time on the diagrams, and the
			values under investigation in the pane. Sliders elsewhere in the Lab are
			the browser's own, tinted lime, with a label and the value beside it. They
			work, but nothing about them belongs to the brand.
		</p>
		<p>
			A first round drew the slider in the brand's shapes: a cut-corner thumb on
			a tall rail, a filled gauge, and a ruler with a marker. Each lost what
			makes a slider recognisable, a thin track and a handle to drag, so it was
			set aside.
		</p>
		<p>
			A second round kept that anatomy, with a lime fill and either a round or a
			cut-corner handle. The round handle was chosen, but lime on the fill, the
			value and the handle together was too much brand colour for a control that
			sits beside the diagrams.
		</p>
		<p>
			A third round kept lime on the knob alone, with a grey fill and a white
			value. It was calm but lifeless: the slider stopped looking like part of
			the instrument.
		</p>
		<p>
			This round uses lime sparingly: the fill up to the value and the knob, and
			nothing else. The label and value stay neutral, and the unfilled track
			takes the accepted faint lime tint under a pointer. Both keep the native
			input underneath, so arrow keys, touch and screen readers behave as
			before, with a 44px touch height. A uses a 4px fill and a solid knob; B a
			2px fill and a lime ring.
		</p>
		<BrandSliderComparison client:load />
		<h3>Accepted: ring</h3>
		<p>
			B was accepted. The slider is a faint 2px track; lime marks the part up to
			the value and the knob, a lime ring with a dark centre. The label and
			value are neutral. Under a pointer the unfilled track takes the faint lime
			tint, as the toggle does. The current instrument's time slider, in 02.7,
			uses it.
		</p>
		<p class="limits">Study-local: not in the shared styles yet.</p>
	</section>

	<section aria-labelledby="burns">
		<h2 id="burns">02.6 / Three burns, and how the input reads</h2>
		<p>
			The instrument owns a small set of input programs, all 14 seconds long; a
			study picks one and never writes its own. Each answers a different kind of
			question:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>Course:</strong>
				the original mixed program: burns, coasts, single-engine turns and
				restarts.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Turn:</strong>
				6 s on both engines, 2 s left only, 1 s coast, then both engines to the
				end. Acceleration, one committed turn, and the burn out of it.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Weave:</strong>
				both, left, right, repeating; each round's steps are 0.3 s longer than
				the last (0.3, 0.6, 0.9 s and on) until 14 s, so response is tested from
				the quickest taps to held inputs.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			Picking a burn is a three-way choice, so the switcher is the accepted
			toggle split into three equal parts, labelled B1, B2 and B3 for the
			programs above. Two codings for the input strip were compared: one colour
			per input, or lime lanes where the upper lane is the left engine and the
			lower the right. The lanes were chosen; they read well and add no hues
			beside the vehicles' trace colours.
		</p>
		<p>
			The switcher and the lanes are in the current instrument, in 02.7. The
			switcher sits on the strip it chooses, just left of the lanes, so the
			lanes stay on the traces' time axis. The burn is part of the configuration
			document, so a switched burn is copied back with the values. With the
			strip, the toggles and the slider all in lime, the instrument overuses the
			brand colour. Five tones for the lit lanes are compared below, from the
			current lime to grey.
		</p>
		<BurnStripComparison client:load />
		<h3>Accepted: burn switcher and input lanes</h3>
		<p>
			The burn strip is decided. Three 14-second burns, B1 to B3, chosen with a
			compact three-part toggle that sits on the strip just left of the lanes.
			The lanes show the left engine above and the right below; a lit lane is
			soft white (white at 70%), leaving lime to the controls. The current
			instrument, in 02.7, uses it.
		</p>
		<p class="limits">Study-local: not in the shared styles yet.</p>
	</section>

	<section aria-labelledby="current">
		<h2 id="current">02.7 / The instrument with the decisions so far</h2>
		<p>
			Each earlier section keeps the instrument as it was reviewed there. This
			one carries every accepted decision: the plan view's two-option toggle
			(02.4), the ring slider for time (02.5), and the burn switcher with soft
			white input lanes (02.6), on the 4px vertical rhythm.
		</p>
		<div class="bleed">
			<LabInstrumentConfigDemo027 client:load />
		</div>
	</section>

	<section aria-labelledby="look">
		<h2 id="look">02.8 / The path tool's edge and material</h2>
		<p>Fixed for the path tool, not open here:</p>
		<ul>
			<li>Its plot is square, 1:1.</li>
			<li>
				Its width is one 50% column of the iPad screen, with sensible padding.
			</li>
			<li>It has the scale switch.</li>
			<li>It shows its numbers: the area shown, the grid and the scale.</li>
			<li>It shows the area of space in relation to the path.</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			Within those, this step asks how the plot separates from what is around
			it. The first round compared a framed panel with no frame; this round
			varies what the edge is made of. The design system has no elevation, so
			two study-local steps are tried. Mixing the app's ivory white into the
			background pulled them towards a neutral grey, and keeping only the
			background's faint chroma left them close to neutral, so they keep its hue
			(290) and raise chroma with lightness: elevation 1 is lch(5.5% 10 290),
			elevation 2 lch(11% 15 290). The example area below is elevation 1; the
			plot is elevation 2.
		</p>
		<p>
			Of five edges tried (white or brand border; white, brand or black shadow),
			the black shadow was accepted: the plot casts a soft shadow downwards, 8px
			down with a 24px blur.
		</p>
		<p>
			Lightness steps alone read as slightly lighter blue-black, not as anything
			in a space instrument. The switch above the one path tool now changes the
			material of both levels, all on the background's hue of 290:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>Tinted void:</strong>
				the lch steps above, kept as the reference.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Lit plate:</strong>
				machined panels lit from the upper left, as the engine studies light
				their parts: a soft diagonal gradient and a thin highlight on each
				level's upper-left edge.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Glass over space:</strong>
				a faint starfield behind tinted glass; the plot is denser glass that
				blurs the stars behind it.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Instrument display:</strong>
				a flat console holding a display with faint scanlines on the 4px rhythm
				and a darkened edge.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<div class="wide">
			<PathToolComparison028 client:load />
		</div>
		<h3>Accepted: instrument display</h3>
		<p>
			The path tool is a display set into a console. Elevation 1, the console,
			is lch(5.5% 10 290); elevation 2, the display, carries faint scanlines on
			the 4px rhythm between lch(11% 15 290) and lch(9% 15 290), a darkened
			edge, a thin dark bezel, and the black shadow beneath it.
		</p>
		<p class="limits">
			Study-local and still in time; drag the plot in default scale. Telling the
			vehicles apart is not part of this step.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section aria-labelledby="readout">
		<h2 id="readout">02.9 / Numbers on the display</h2>
		<p>
			With the path tool a display, its title and numbers move onto it. A first
			try stacked them small in the top-left corner; they now read larger, on
			one line in the display's bottom-right corner: the name, the area shown,
			the grid spacing and the scale. Nothing sits above the display any more,
			so the tool is the square itself.
		</p>
		<div class="wide">
			<PathToolComparison client:load />
		</div>
		<p class="limits">
			Study-local and still in time; drag the display in default scale. The
			numbers read over the grid and paths without a backing.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section aria-labelledby="graphs">
		<h2 id="graphs">02.10 / The time graphs: the basics</h2>
		<p>
			Speed, acceleration and turn acceleration are one graph component used
			three times; they differ only in name, unit and range. They share the path
			tool's display material and shadow, but they are graphs, so they need
			measurements and every label outside the data:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				A value axis on the instrument's fixed scale, never fitted: about five
				ticks with values and gridlines, a stronger zero line where the range
				crosses zero.
			</li>
			<li>
				A time axis, 0–14 s with a gridline and a label every second, the same
				on every graph: the component is one complete graph, shown three times.
			</li>
			<li>
				A title row above the plot: the measure and its unit on the left, and on
				the right the time and each vehicle's value at the cursor, in its
				colour. A warning appears there if a value exceeds the fixed scale.
			</li>
			<li>The time cursor: a vertical line with a dot on each trace.</li>
			<li>
				Margins on the 4px grid: 28px for the title row, 52px for the value
				labels, 24px for the seconds. Each graph is 176px tall, so three with
				16px gaps leave 112px of the path tool's 672px for the lanes and slider.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<div class="wide">
			<TraceGraphsExample client:load />
		</div>
		<p class="limits">
			Study-local and still in time (7 s). A first round put the name and range
			on the display over the data, with no axes; it is not kept. The lanes and
			slider are not drawn here, and the burn switcher's place beside them is
			open.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section aria-labelledby="composed">
		<h2 id="composed">02.11 / The composed view</h2>
		<p>
			The path tool and the time graph put together on the instrument's screen.
			The screen is the console, elevation 1. The left column holds the path
			tool at its square size with the burn row under it, as accepted in 02.6:
			the compact B1–B3 switcher, then the selected burn's lanes in soft white.
			The right column holds the graph three times, 208px each with 24px between
			them, with the time slider under it. Both rows are the full column width
			and 44px tall, 24px below their block, so the two columns end level; the
			slider's knob runs along the graphs' time axis. Below both columns, one
			row across the screen names the vehicles: a short line in each one's
			colour, then its label, for the two to six vehicles a configuration holds.
			The pane is not shown. The screen fills the window and snaps into place.
		</p>
		<p>
			Decided here: every graph scales its value axis to fit the data, rounded
			out to a nice value, and a measure that can go negative stays symmetric
			about zero. This replaces the fixed scales of 02.10: nothing is clipped,
			and values are compared by reading the axis rather than the height of a
			line. The path tool keeps its own switch between fitting all paths and the
			default scale.
		</p>
		<div class="bleed">
			<InstrumentView client:load />
		</div>
		<p class="limits">
			Study-local. Switching the burn recalculates every diagram; drag the
			slider or the path tool in default scale.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section aria-labelledby="readiness">
		<h2 id="readiness">03 / Readiness: what ships as v1</h2>
		<p>
			The accepted design is the composed view of 02.11 with the pane on top:
			the path tool and its burn row in the left column, the time graph three
			times and the time slider in the right, the legend across both, all on the
			console and display materials. The pane overlay is specified but not yet
			drawn: elevation 3 on the same progression as the two below it (lightness
			up 5.5 and chroma up 5 per step, so lch(16.5% 20 290)), 45% of the screen
			wide, and an 11% opaque backdrop over the rest. What the pane holds is
			filled in by the agent for each study.
		</p>
		<h3>Ready to reuse</h3>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>Calculation:</strong>
				paths come from the game's own engine function, with each vehicle's part
				and ion values passed in, so a study's changes never touch the game's
				definitions.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Configuration document:</strong>
				one validated schema: vehicles as loadouts plus sparse changes, and the
				burn. It already round-trips through Copy and Load in the 02.3 pane.
			</li>
			<li><strong>Burns:</strong> B1–B3 as data, each exactly 14 seconds.</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Diagrams and controls:</strong>
				the path tool, the time graph, the burn lanes, the two- and three-part
				toggle and the ring slider, each a separate component in its accepted
				form, plus the shared display material.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Pane behaviour:</strong>
				shown or hidden from a corner button, Escape and focus return, as
				specified in 01 and built in 02.3.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<h3>Required before v1 ships</h3>
		<ol>
			<li>
				<strong>One component from one document.</strong>
				The composed view is wired to the study's example vehicles. It has to
				take a configuration document, validate it, and derive the paths,
				graphs, burn and legend from it.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>The pane overlay.</strong>
				Drawn to the specification above, with a slot the host fills. Its
				default content is the existing value editor and the document's Copy and
				Load, set in the accepted controls.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Where it lives.</strong>
				The components move out of the study folder into the Lab's own
				components; the frozen copies stay with the study as its record.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>A Lab page and a study embed.</strong>
				The Lab page fills its container; in a study the instrument takes the
				full window and snaps into place, as on this page.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>How agents call it.</strong>
				A specification of the contract and one rule in the design-study skill:
				motion, engine and hull comparisons use this instrument and its
				configuration document rather than new charts.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Design-system pages.</strong>
				The new components join the shared design system, each with a Lab
				reference page as the buttons have: the toggle and the ring slider with
				the inputs, beside the buttons; the path tool and the time graph on a
				Graphs page; the burn row, the legend and the pane overlay on a Lab
				components page; the elevation steps and the console and display
				materials with the foundations.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Tests.</strong>
				Unit tests for the burns, the schema and the calculation; one browser
				test of the composed view, the burn switch, the slider and the pane.
			</li>
		</ol>
		<h3>Provisional, not blocking</h3>
		<ul>
			<li>
				The path tool's default scale (0.2 px/u) has not been tuned. Time moves
				in 1/60 s samples.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<h3>Risks and limits</h3>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>Fidelity:</strong>
				gravity, power limits, and changes to part mass or chassis values are
				not in the calculation. A difference shown here may not exist in flight;
				the specification has to say so.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Comparison across studies:</strong>
				with every graph scaled to its data, two studies are compared by reading
				their axes, not by laying the lines over each other.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Readability:</strong>
				the whole screen scales with the window, so text gets small on small
				windows. Nothing has been checked on a physical iPad.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Elevation 3:</strong>
				its value is an assumed continuation of the progression and has not been
				seen yet.
			</li>
		</ul>
	</section>

	<section aria-labelledby="integration">
		<h2 id="integration">04 / Integration plan</h2>
		<p>
			Each step leaves the instrument working and can be reviewed on its own.
			This is a plan; building it needs a separate go-ahead.
		</p>
		<ol>
			<li>
				<strong>Data first.</strong>
				Move the configuration schema, the burns and the calculation into one
				Lab module, unchanged, with unit tests: each burn is 14 s, the schema
				accepts the 02.7 document and rejects a broken loadout, and the
				calculation is deterministic.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>The component.</strong>
				Build the Lab instrument from the 02.11 view, the path tool, the time
				graph, the burn lanes, the toggle, the slider and the display material,
				taking a configuration document. Check it renders the 02.7 document
				exactly as 02.11 does.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>The pane.</strong>
				Add the overlay to the specification, with a slot and the default value
				editor and Copy and Load. Review point: the designer judges elevation 3
				and the backdrop in place.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Design-system promotion and pages.</strong>
				The toggle (two and three parts, with the lime-tint hover) and the ring
				slider become shared input styles beside the buttons; the elevation
				steps and the console and display materials become foundation tokens.
				Each component gets a Lab reference page with its states, as the buttons
				have:
				<ul>
					<li>
						<strong>Inputs:</strong>
						the buttons, the toggle and the ring slider together.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Graphs:</strong>
						the path tool and the time graph.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Lab components:</strong>
						the burn row, the legend and the pane overlay.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Foundations:</strong>
						the elevation steps and the console and display materials.
					</li>
				</ul>
				Review point: the designer checks each page against its accepted
				section.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>The Lab page.</strong>
				Add the instrument to the Lab with a starting document and Load, filling
				the container. Review point: the designer uses it for one real
				comparison.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>The study embed and the contract.</strong>
				Add the full-window embed for study pages. Write the instrument's
				specification: the document, the burns, the views and the decisions
				above, with targets kept apart from what is built. Add the rule to the
				design-study skill, and the new inputs, graphs and foundations to the
				design guide and the design-system specification.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Verification.</strong>
				Lint, type and Astro checks, the unit tests, a browser test of the view,
				burn switch, slider and pane, a render check of every new reference
				page, and a human review on the iPad for readability. Mark the study
				integrated only after these pass and the review is recorded.
			</li>
		</ol>
		<h3>Follow-ups, outside v1</h3>
		<ul>
			<li>
				Add part mass, chassis values, gravity and power limits to the
				calculation.
			</li>
			<li>More burns, if studies need them.</li>
			<li>Tune the path tool's default scale.</li>
		</ul>
		<h3>v1 as built</h3>
		<p>
			The shipped instrument, embedded as any study embeds it, from the starting
